# 5. What Problems VFXNOW Addresses

VFXNOW should be understood through the production hardware and compute problems it helps organizations address.

## 5.1 Insufficient compute capacity

A production workload can exceed the capacity of the systems currently available to the team.

The relevant question is usually not whether a faster computer would be desirable, but where additional CPU, GPU, memory, or system capacity materially changes the production constraint.

VFXNOW can provide access to higher-performance systems when the existing hardware is no longer appropriate for the workload.

## 5.2 Temporary increases in production demand

Hardware demand does not always remain constant across a production.

Teams may require additional systems during specific phases of work without wanting the same capacity permanently.

VFXNOW can support temporary scaling when additional hardware is justified by the production requirement.

## 5.3 Workloads that require specific configurations

Professional creative applications can place very different demands on workstation or compute architecture.

VFXNOW can provide systems configured around the requirements of the intended workload rather than treating hardware as a generic specification exercise.

## 5.4 Hardware constraints affecting production work

When performance limitations begin changing how artists or technical teams approach the work, the hardware has become an operational constraint rather than simply a technical specification.

VFXNOW can help teams address those constraints through more appropriate production hardware.

## 5.5 Deployment and configuration during active production

Production teams may need additional hardware without creating a long configuration or deployment process.

VFXNOW's role includes providing systems that are prepared for professional production use and relevant software environments.

