# 12. Editorial Boundaries

VFXNOW content should not become:

* consumer hardware buying guides
* gaming-PC content
* generic “best computer” articles
* broad hardware specification lists without production context
* explanations of why faster hardware is better
* repeated descriptions of production pressure
* long software-name lists
* generic productivity content
* Managed Services infrastructure articles
* Software Services workflow-system articles
* marketing copy disguised as technical editorial content

Avoid proving production expertise by naming every possible application, workload, artist role, or hardware specification.

The analysis should demonstrate that the writer understands the relationship between the workload and the system.

---

# 13. Performance and Specification Discipline

Hardware content can easily become specification-driven.

Specifications should be included only when they materially affect the argument.

Do not list:

* CPU cores
* GPU models
* memory capacities
* storage speeds
* benchmark scores

simply to make an article feel technical.

When a specification matters, explain **why that particular constraint changes the workload or production decision**.

Likewise, avoid treating benchmark performance as equivalent to production performance unless the comparison genuinely reflects the workload being discussed.

The strongest hardware analysis should remain workload-aware.

---

# 14. VFXNOW Positioning in Editorial Content

VFXNOW should appear as a knowledgeable production-hardware partner rather than as the solution to every hardware problem.

The analysis should establish credibility.

Avoid:

* repeated VFXNOW mentions
* paragraphs that interrupt technical reasoning to describe VFXNOW offerings
* generic claims that VFXNOW “understands deadlines”
* repeated descriptions of production-ready hardware
* sales language about performance or speed without a specific technical basis

In most editorial articles, one natural VFXNOW connection near the conclusion is sufficient.

